<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 4.1: Right Triangles</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  <sidebyside widths="25% 25% 30%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The unit circle with central angle t and x coordinate cos(t) and y coordinate sin(t).
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-1-0a">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.2,1.2,1.2)">
            <axes decorations="no"/>
            <circle radius="1" center="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0.4*pi)))" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <point p="(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0.4*pi))"><m>(\cos(t), \sin(t))</m></point>
            <point p="(cos(0),sin(0))"><m>(1,0)</m></point>
            <label anchor="(0.1,0.6)"><m>1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.1,0.1)"><m>t</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The unit circle with central angle t and x coordinate cos(t) and y coordinate sin(t).
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-1-0b">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.2,1.2,1.2)">
            <axes decorations="no"/>
            <circle radius="1" center="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0.4*pi)))" stroke="red" thickness="3"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0)))" stroke="red" thickness="5"/>
            <line endpoints="((cos(0.4*pi),0),(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0.4*pi)))" stroke="red" thickness="3"></line>
            <point p="(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0.4*pi))"></point>
            <point p="(cos(0.4*pi),sin(0))"></point>
            <label anchor="(0.1,0.6)"><m>1</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.1,0.1)"><m>\theta</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.55,0.4)"><m>\sin(\theta)</m></label>
            <label anchor="(0.2,-0.15)"><m>\cos(\theta)</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    
    <p>Pythagorean Theorem for right triangles:
<md>a^2+b^2 = c^2</md>
Fundamental Trig Identity:
<md>\cos^2(\theta) + \sin^2(\theta) = 1</md></p>
  </sidebyside>

  <p workspace=".5in">Angles of a triangle:</p>
  <p workspace=".5in">Right Triangles:</p>
  <p workspace="1.5in"><term>Similar Triangles:</term> One triangle is a magnification of the other.  Same angles!</p>
  <p workspace="1in"><term>(Brief and incomplete) explanation of trig inverse:</term></p>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 4.1.2</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider right triangle <m>OPQ</m>
 given in the figure below, and assume that the length of the hypotenuse is <m>OP=r</m>
 for some constant <m>r&gt;1</m>.
 The point <m>M</m>
 lies on the line segment between 
 <m>O</m> and <m>P</m> in such a way that the length <m>OM=1</m> , and point <m>N</m>
 lies on the line segment between <m>O</m> and <m>Q</m>
 so that the angle <m>\angle ONM</m> 
 is a right angle, as pictured. In addition, assume that point <m>O</m>
 corresponds to <m>(0,0)</m>,
 point <m>Q</m>
 to <m>(x,0)</m>,
 and point <m>P</m>
 to <m>(x,y)</m>
 so that <m>OQ=x</m>
 and <m>PQ=y</m>.
 Finally, let <m>\theta</m>
 be the measure of angle <m>\angle POQ</m>.</p>
          <image width="55%" margins="0% 45%">
      <shortdescription>
        Two right triangles which share vertex 𝑂: a larger one, △𝑂⁢𝑃⁢𝑄 whose hypotenuse is r, and a smaller one, △𝑂⁢𝑀⁢𝑁 whose hypotenuse is 1
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-1-2">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,150)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,13,5)">
            <triangle vertices="((0,0),(12,0),(12,4))"  stroke="blue" labels="O,Q,P"
              show-vertices="yes" point-fill="orange"/>
              <triangle vertices="((0,0),(4,0),(4,4/3))"  stroke="blue" labels="O,N,M"
              show-vertices="yes" point-fill="orange"/>
              <angle-marker points="((12,4),(12,0),(0,0))" stroke="red"/>
              <angle-marker points="((4,4/3),(4,0),(0,0))" stroke="red"/>
              <label anchor="(1.8,.29)"><m>\theta</m></label>
              <label anchor="(1.8,1.29)"><m>1</m></label>
              <label anchor="(12.4,2.)"><m>y</m></label>
              <label anchor="(4.3,-1)"><m>x</m></label>
              <label anchor="(3.8,2.8)"><m>r</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Explain why <m>\triangle OPQ</m>
 and <m>\triangle OMN</m>
 are similar triangles.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          One is a magnification of the other.  Same angles, one of 
          the sides are some <m>k</m> times larger than the other.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the value of the ratio 
 <m>\frac{OP}{OM}</m>?
 According to the definition of similar triangles, what does this tell you about the ratios 
 <m>\frac{OQ}{ON}</m>
 and <m>\frac{PQ}{MN}</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          <m>\dfrac{OP}{OM}=\dfrac{larger-hyp}{smaller-hyp}=\dfrac{r}{1}=r</m>. So the 
          larger triangle is <m>r</m> times larger than the smaller triangle!
          So all the rations must be the same since they are similar triangles:
          <md>
            \dfrac{OQ}{ON}=\dfrac{larger-horiz}{smaller-horiz} = \dfrac{r}{1}.
          </md>
          <md>
            \dfrac{PQ}{MN}=\dfrac{larger-vert}{smaller-vert} = \dfrac{r}{1}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the value of <m>ON</m>
 in terms of <m>\theta</m>?
 What is the value of <m>MN</m>
 in terms of <m>\theta</m>? (Think unit circle! Top of page!)</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          Since the hypotenuse is <m>1</m>, we can embed this triangle into the unit 
          circle!  And the y-coordinate will correspond to the vertical 
          side of the triangle and is given by <m>\sin(\theta)</m>.  Similarly
          the x-coordinate on the unit circle will correspond to the 
          horizontal side of the triangle and is given by <m>\cos(\theta)</m>.
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use your conclusions in (b) and (c) to express the values of <m>x</m>
 and <m>y</m>
 in terms of <m>r</m>
 and <m>\theta</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          Since we know all three sides of the smaller triangle, and the larger triangle sides 
          are <m>r</m> times as larger.  So the 
          hypotenuse is <m>r * 1</m> or just <m>r</m>, 
          the vertical side is <m>r*\sin(\theta)</m> so <m>y=r\sin(\theta)</m>, 
          and the horizontal side is <m>r*\cos(\theta)</m> so <m>x=r\cos(\theta)</m>.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    This leads us to some cool relationships if we think of 
                    <m>x</m> as the adjacent side (since it is next to the angle <m>\theta</m>)
                    and we think of <m>y</m> as the opposite side since it is opposite of <m>\theta</m>
                    and <m>r</m> as the hypotenuse (since it is) this leads us to the 
                    relationships on the following page.
                  </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Ratios in right triangles</title>
    <sidebyside widths="60% 30%">
      <stack>
        <p>In a right triangle where one of the non-right angles is <m>\theta</m>:</p>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>“adj” is the length of the leg adjacent to <m>\theta</m>,</p></li>
            <li><p>“opp” is the length the side opposite <m>\theta</m>,</p></li>
            <li><p>“hyp” is the length of the hypotenuse,</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p><md>\cos(\theta) = \frac{\text{adj}}{\text{hyp}} \ \ \ \text{ and } \ \ \ \sin(\theta) = \frac{\text{opp}}{\text{hyp}}</md></p>
      </stack>
          <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A right triangle with a non-right angle theta and sides labeled adjacent, opposite, and hypotenuse.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-1-ratios">
        <diagram dimensions="(200,200)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1.5,13,19)">
            <triangle vertices="((0,0),(12,0),(12,18))" stroke="blue" 
              show-vertices="no"/>
              <angle-marker points="((12,4),(12,0),(0,0))" stroke="red"/>
              <label anchor="(1.8,1.29)"><m>\theta</m></label>
              <label anchor="(12.8,9.)"><m>opp</m></label>
              <label anchor="(6.3,-1)"><m>adj</m></label>
              <label anchor="(5.8,10.8)"><m>hyp</m></label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </sidebyside>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 4.1.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>In each of the following scenarios involving a right triangle, determine the exact values of as many of the remaining side lengths and angle measures (in radians) that you can. If there are quantities that you cannot determine, explain why. For every prompt, draw a labeled diagram of the situation.  (Pictures help!)</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>A right triangle with hypotenuse <m>7</m>
 and one non-right angle of measure 
 <m>\frac{\pi}{7}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          Would recommend drawing a picture and sticking <m>\frac{\pi}{7}</m>
          in one corner of the triangle (not the right angle but one of the others)
          and labeling which side is opposite that angle, and which is adjacent to 
          that angle. 
          </p><p>
            We'll call the other non-right angle <m>B</m> for no reason 
            whatsoever.  Since the 2 non-right angles have to add up 
            to <m>90^\circ = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>.  That is 
          <m>{B+\dfrac{\pi}{7} = \dfrac{\pi}{2}}</m>, or <m>{B = \frac{\pi}2 - \frac{\pi}7=\frac{5\pi}{14}}</m>.  
          </p><p>
          Let's think about the adjacent side, and we have the hypotenuse and 
          the angle.  So we can look at cosine:
          <md>
            \cos(\frac{\pi}7) = \frac{adj}{7}.
          </md>
          Then we can solve for <m>adj</m> by multiplying both sides by 7: 
          <m>{\adj = 7\cos(\frac{\pi}{7})}</m> (exact) or <m>\approx 6.307</m>.
          </p><p>
          There are two ways to find the third side (possibly more).  Pick one, 
          you don't need both, but here they are just because:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use sine!  <m>\sin(\frac{\pi}{7}) = \frac{opp}{7}</m> so 
                <m>{opp = 7\sin(\frac{\pi}{7})}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Pythagorean Theorem!  
                <md>
                  (opp)^2 + (7\cos(\frac{\pi}{7}))^2 = 7^2
                </md>
                which can be solved by 
                <md>
                  opp = \sqrt{49 -(7\cos(\frac{\pi}{7}))^2}
                </md>
                which doesn't simplify nicely.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Either will get you approximately <m>opp \approx 3.072</m>.
  
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>A right triangle with non-right angle <m>\alpha</m>
 that satisfies 
<m>\sin(\alpha)=\frac35</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          One way to think of this is <m>\sin(\alpha)=\frac{3}5 =\frac{opp}{hyp}</m>
          so we can think of the opposite side as 3 and the hypotenuse as 5.  However, 
          we can also think of the opposite side as <m>3/5</m> and the hypotenuse as 1...
          or the opposite side as 6 and the hypotenuse as 10<ellipsis/> or<ellipsis/> 
          Really any multiple.  So we cannot determine the side lengths, only the ratios.  
          Any multiple of a 3-4-5 right triangle will satisfy this.
        </p>
        <p>
          Angles though we can do a little.  Since 
          <m>{\sin(\alpha)=\dfrac{3}{5}}</m>, we can essentially take the inverse (
            which we will talk more about so for now we are being hand-wavey
          ), to get 
          <m>{\alpha=\arcsin\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right)}</m> radians.  This is an exact answer.
          It is also approximately <m>\alpha\approx 0.6435</m> radians.
          </p><p>
          There are multiple ways to get the other angle, which I'll name <m>B</m>. 
          Here's the simplest:
                Non-right angles have to add to 90 degrees or <m>\pi/2</m>.
                <m>{B+\arcsin\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right) = \frac{\pi}{2}}</m> which means
                <m>{B = \frac{\pi}{2} - \arcsin\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right)}</m>.
     (<m>B\approx 0.9273</m>.)
        </p>
    
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>A right triangle where one of the non-right angles has measure 
 <m>1.2</m> and the hypotenuse has length <m>2.7</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          The other non-right angle <m>B</m> is found from 
          <m>{B+1.2 = \frac{\pi}{2}}</m>, or <m>{B=\frac\pi2-1.2}</m> (this is 
          in radians). 
          </p><p>
          Then <m>\cos(1.2) = \frac{adj}{2.7}</m>, or <m>{adj = 2.7\cos(1.2)}</m>
          or about 0.978.
          </p><p>
          There are multiple ways to find the other side.  
          One of the cleanest is <m>{\sin(1.2)=\frac{opp}{2.7}}</m>, 
          which gives <m>{opp = 2.7\sin(1.2)}</m> or about 2.517.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>A right triangle with hypotenuse <m>13</m>
 and one leg of length <m>6.5</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          The other leg can be found using Pythagorean Theorem:
          <md>(adj)^2+6.5^2 = 13^2</md>, or <m>{adj=\sqrt{13^2-6.5^2}}\approx 11.26</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          We put an angle <m>A</m> in the triangle so that it is 
          opposite from 6.5.  Then we can use
          <m>\sin(A)=\dfrac{6.5}{13}</m>, so
          we can find the angle by taking 
          <m>A=\arcsin(\dfrac{6.5}{13})</m> (exact). We could stop here
          for this but if you're interested, <m>\frac{6.5}{13}=\frac12</m>, 
          and so we have <m>sin(A)=\frac12</m>, and this is something we can find 
          from the unit circle (<m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m>).  
          </p><p>
          The other angle <m>B</m> would satisfy <m>A+B=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, so 
          <m>B = \dfrac{\pi}{2}-\arcsin(\dfrac{6.5}{13})</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>A right triangle with legs of length <m>5</m>
 and <m>12</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
         The other leg can be found using Pythagorean Theorem:
          <md>5^2+12^2 = (hyp)^2</md>, or <m>{hyp=13}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
         The angle <m>A</m> we'll take to be the one opposite the leg of length
          <m>5</m>. Then <m>\sin(A)=\frac5{13}</m>, and we can get 
          <m>A=\arcsin(\frac{5}{13})\approx 0.395</m> radians.
        </p>
        <p>
         Then the last angle <m>B</m> we can find from <m>A+B=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, or 
         <m>B = \frac{\pi}{2} - \arcsin(\frac{5}{13})</m>.
        
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>A right triangle where one of the non-right angles has measure 
 <m>\frac{\pi}{5}</m>
 and the leg opposite this angle has length <m>4</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S4-1-soln">
        <p>
          The other non-right angle we'll call <m>B</m> has to have 
          <m>B+\frac{\pi}{5} = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, so 
          <m>B = \dfrac{\pi}{2}-\dfrac{\pi}{5}=\dfrac{3\pi}{10}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then 
          <m>\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{5}\right)=\dfrac{4}{\text{hyp}}</m>, which 
          we can solve by multiplying both sides by the hypotenuse:
          <md>
            (hyp)\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{5}\right)  = {4}
          </md>
          or <m>{hyp = \frac{4}{\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{5}\right)}}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Similarly <m>\cos\left(\dfrac{\pi}{5}\right) = \dfrac{adj}{\text{hyp}} = \dfrac{adj}{\frac{4}{\sin\left(\dfrac{\pi}{5}\right)}}</m>
          which is a little gross but we can get <md>
            adj = \frac{4\cos(\pi/5)}{\sin(\pi/5)}.
          </md>
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Missing info in right triangles</title>
    <tabular>
      <row>
        <cell><term>Known</term></cell>
        <cell><term>Can Find</term></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Two leg lengths</cell>
        <cell>Third leg length</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>One non-right angle</cell>
        <cell>Remaining non-right angle</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>One non-right angle &amp; hypotenuse</cell>
        <cell>Remaining angle and both leg lengths</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 4.1.4</title>
    <statement workspace="1in">
      <p>We want to determine the distance between two points <m>A</m>
 and <m>B</m>
 that are directly across from one another on opposite sides of a river, as pictured in the figure. We mark the locations of those points and walk <m>50</m>
 meters downstream from <m>B</m>
 to point <m>P</m> 
 and use the bubble level app on your phone to measure <m>\angle BPA</m>.
 If the measure of <m>\angle BPA</m>
 is <m>56.4^{\circ}</m>,
 how wide is the river? What other information about the situation can you determine?</p>
          <image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
      <shortdescription>
        A right triangle with a non-right angle 56.4.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-4-1-4">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="18">
          <coordinates bbox="(-2,-1.5,14,20)">
            <triangle vertices="((0,0),(12,0),(12,18))"  stroke="blue" 
              show-vertices="yes" dash="4 4" labels="P,B,A" />
              <angle-marker points="((12,4),(12,0),(0,0))" stroke="red"/>
              <label anchor="(1.9,1.)"><m>56.4^\circ</m></label>
              <label anchor="(6.3,-1)"><m>50</m></label>
              <line endpoints="((-2,0),(14,0))"/>
              <line endpoints="((-2,18),(14,18))"/>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S4-1-soln">
      <p>
        If we call the vertical side (the width of the river) <m>y</m>
        and the hypotenuse <m>h</m>, then we are looking for <m>y</m> which is 
        the width of the river.  For the angle that we are given, we want the opposite side <m>y</m>
        and we have the adjacent side <m>50</m>, and we don't know the hypotenuse.
        Ok well sine uses the opposite side and since we want the opposite side 
        let's try that and try to solve for <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          \sin(56.4) = \frac{y}{h}
        </md>
        or rearranging <md>
          y = h\sin(56.4).
        </md>
        But now the problem is we don't know <m>h</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, we know the adjacent side and an angle, so cosine is much more 
        promising:
        <md>
          \cos(56.4) = \frac{50}{h}
        </md>
        or solving for <m>h</m> we have <m>{h\cos(56.4) = 50}</m> or 
        <md>
          h = \frac{50}{\cos(56.4)}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        How we can go back and plug in <m>h</m> to find <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          y = h\sin(56.4) = (\frac{50}{\cos(56.4)})\sin(56.4).
        </md>
        It turns out the river is approximately <m>75.26</m> meters wide.
      </p>
      <p>
        Note we can also find the other non-right angle <m>B</m> by doing
        <m>{B+56.4 = 90}</m>, or in other words <m>B = 33.6^\circ</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        It would be lovely if we had a function that used the opposite 
        and adjacent sides and didn't use the hypotenuse...
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

</handout>
